  }
  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .package-dining-card-item {
      display: block;
    }
  }
  /* The middle format sits a little lower so the row reads as a composed
       triptych rather than three identical tiles in a row. */
  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .package-dining-card-item:nth-child(2) {
      margin-top: clamp(20px, 3vw, 46px);
    }
  }
  /* Reveal stagger, triptych only. The three cards enter left-to-right one
       after another rather than as a single block — main.js reveals all three
       the moment the row crosses the threshold (see its own comment for why
       the middle card can't be left to its own intersection), and this is what
       spaces them out once they do.

       Deliberately CSS rather than the inline `style="--d: …"` these cards used
       to carry: an inline custom property beats every stylesheet rule at every
       width (CLAUDE.md bug #10), so a mobile query could never have cancelled
       it. Below 980px the cards are stacked or 2-up and reveal individually as
       each scrolls into view, where a delay is just lag on a card the visitor
       is already looking at — so the stagger has to be able to switch off, and
       living here is what lets it. */
  /* Slowed deliberately, and much further than a normal reveal: this is the
       one moment the page states outright that there are three options, and
       the cards arriving one at a time over a couple of seconds is what makes
       that land rather than three tiles appearing at once.

       Timing: each card takes ~1.1s to fade and ~1.2s to settle, and they
       start 600ms apart — so the sequence opens immediately, the third card
       begins at 1.2s, and the whole thing resolves around 2.4s. The gap
       between starts is longer than half a card's own fade on purpose; any
       tighter and they read as overlapping rather than as one-then-the-next.

       Declared at 0,2,0 (.package-dining-card-item .package-dining-card), not
       as a bare .package-dining-card — this media query sits ABOVE the base
       .package-dining-card rule in the file, so an equal-specificity override
       here would lose on source order (CLAUDE.md bug #14: check what is
       already targeting the element, and at what weight, before assuming a
       later-looking rule wins).

       The full transition list is restated, not just the durations, because
       `transition` is a shorthand: setting transition-duration alone would
       stretch border-color to 1.1s too and turn the hover border glow into a
       slow smear. The delay is restated for the same reason — the shorthand
       resets it, which is precisely the bug that kept the original stagger
       from ever running — but PER ENTRY, on opacity and transform only.
       A standalone `transition-delay` longhand here would hand the --d
       values below to border-color as well, which is what made the hover
       border on cards 2 and 3 arrive 0.6s and 1.2s late. See the matching
       note on .package-dining-card's own transition, and CLAUDE.md bug #12. */
  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .package-dining-card-item .package-dining-card {
      transition:
        opacity 1.1s var(--ease) var(--d, 0ms),
        transform 1.2s var(--ease) var(--d, 0ms),
        border-color 0.5s var(--ease);
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .package-dining-card-item:nth-child(2) .package-dining-card {
      --d: 600ms;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .package-dining-card-item:nth-child(3) .package-dining-card {
      --d: 1200ms;
    }
  }
  /* Two-up tablet tier: a leftover third card centers itself on its own row
     rather than forcing a premature drop to one column. The floating "or"
     disc and the triptych's middle-card offset (980px+ below) both assume a
     deterministic three-in-a-row layout, so this tier keeps the same
     stacked/inline "or" treatment as the single-column layout instead.

     621px lower bound (was 700px): this tier now runs the whole way down to
     the mobile breakpoint, so the section has exactly two boundaries — 620 and
     980 — instead of an island in the middle. Previously 621-699 fell through
     to the base rule: a single column of full-width stacked cards, and above
     640 the figure reverted from the old mobile 3/2 crop to the base 1/1, so a
     ~660px viewport rendered a ~620px-tall square image inside a 900px+ card
     sitting between two compact layouts.

     Why 700 existed, and why coming below it is safe now: CLAUDE.md bug #20.
     At a 50/50 split the card's own .package-card-link (a .button, deliberately
     flex-shrink:0 with a nowrap label) stops fitting and overflows rather than
     compressing — measured as a ~10px deficit right around 620-626px once
     .package-dining-card-body's own padding came out of the column width, and
     700px was the measured floor for the full-size button. At 621px there is
     ~230px of usable width inside the card body against a full .button's
     ~252px with a long label. The 621-699.98px block further down sizes the
     button to .button-small's own dimensions in that band, bringing it to
     ~222px, which fits. Keep the two together — that button rule is what makes
     this lower bound safe, not an optional polish on top of it. */
  /* 979.98, not 979: paired with the triptych's `min-width: 980px` below, an
     integer bound leaves everything from 979.01 to 979.99 matching neither
     query, so a fractional viewport (browser zoom, fractional device pixel
     ratios, a dragged desktop window) fell through to the base rule and
     showed a single column of full-width stacked cards. Caught after the same
     hole was hit for real at 699.2px on the tier below. The 0.02 offset is
     the Bootstrap convention — browsers round reported widths to two decimal
     places — and is preferred over overlapping the two ranges, which would
     make the outcome depend on source order (CLAUDE.md bug #10).
     The same hole existed on every other paired breakpoint in this file
     (620/621, 639/640, 760/761, 920/921, 980/981) and all of them were closed
     the same way in the same pass — see CLAUDE.md bug #22. Every max-width
     query here now ends .98 below the min-width it hands over to. */
  /* The CLAUDE.md bug #20 guard that lets the 2-up tier above start at 621px
     rather than 700px (see its comment for the measurements). .button-small's
     own dimensions reused rather than a new size invented. Belt-and-braces
     against today's copy — the four visible labels all read "View menu" now,
     which fits either way — but the moment any of them is lengthened again the
     bottom of this band overflows exactly as bug #20 describes. */
  /* The "or" marker: a gold-ringed disc between two formats. Stacked between
     cards on narrow screens, floated into the column gap on wide ones. */
  .package-dining-card-or {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    align-self: center;
    width: 46px;
    height: 46px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--gold-rgb), 0.4);
    background: var(--ink-2);
    color: var(--gold-2);
    font-family: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    flex: none;
  }
  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .package-dining-card-or {
      position: absolute;
      top: clamp(90px, 11vw, 150px);
      left: calc(clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 30px) / -2 - 23px);
      z-index: 2;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .package-dining-card-item:nth-child(2) .package-dining-card-or {
      top: clamp(70px, 8vw, 104px);
    }
  }
  .package-dining-card {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    height: 100%;
    background: var(--ink-2);
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--limestone-rgb), 0.12);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    overflow: hidden;
    /* Restates [data-reveal]'s own opacity/transform transition (same
       specificity, 0,1,0) alongside the new border-color entry — per CLAUDE.md
       bug #2, a same-specificity rule wins wholesale, not per-property, so
       adding border-color alone here would've silently dropped the reveal
       animation. */
    /* And the same bug once more, one property further out: `transition` is a
       shorthand, so it also resets transition-delay to 0s. [data-reveal] sets
       `transition-delay: var(--d, 0ms)` as a separate longhand at the same
       0,1,0 specificity, but it is declared far earlier in this file, so the
       shorthand here won and silently killed the stagger — the triptych's own
       --d values were a no-op from the day the section shipped.

       The delay is therefore restated — but PER ENTRY, on opacity and
       transform only, NOT as a standalone `transition-delay` longhand
       (2026-09-25). A standalone delay applies to every transition on the
       element, border-color included, so the triptych's own stagger
       (--d: 600ms / 1200ms on cards 2 and 3, further down this file) was
       also delaying the hover border: the third card waited 1.2s before its
       border even began to move, then took another 0.5s to arrive. Read as
       a broken hover, and was reported as one.

       Exactly CLAUDE.md bug #12, which records the same mistake on the
       mobile drawer's link stagger and the same fix: put the delay on the
       entries that want it via a custom property and leave the others with
       no var, rather than reaching for the shorthand's delay slot. */
    transition:
      opacity 0.9s var(--ease) var(--d, 0ms),
      transform 1s var(--ease) var(--d, 0ms),
      border-color 0.5s var(--ease);
  }
  /* Whole-card link: the card already reacts to hover everywhere (border glow
     + photo zoom below), so only the small pill at its foot being clickable
     read as a mismatch (same affordance issue as .package-station-card
     elsewhere on this page). One real hit target stretched over the whole
     card, rather than wrapping all of it in an <a> — the card holds an <ol> of
     tier names, and a whole-card <a> would read all of that aloud as part of
     its accessible name. A single <a> with its own concise aria-label
     sidesteps that. The visible pill (.package-card-link) becomes a
     non-interactive, aria-hidden visual echo of the same label. Shared class
     (not scoped to .package-dining-card) since .package-beverage-card below
     wants the identical treatment. */
  .package-card-hit {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 3;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: transparent;
    cursor: pointer;
    appearance: none;
  }
  .package-card-hit:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--gold);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
  @media (hover: none) {
    .package-dining-card {
      border-color: transparent;
      background:
        linear-gradient(var(--ink-2), var(--ink-2)) padding-box,
        var(--gold-gradient-soft) border-box;
    }
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .package-dining-card:hover {
      border-color: rgba(var(--gold-rgb), 0.38);
    }
  }
  /* .package-card-hit sits above everything in the card, so the visible pill
       no longer receives its own native :hover — this restores its fill-on-
       hover (background/color/shadow/lift, sheen sweep, arrow nudge) via the
       card's :hover instead. Transitions still animate smoothly since they're
       declared on .package-card-link itself (via .button); only the trigger
       moved. background-color is a no-op once .button-gradient's opaque
       gradient layer covers it — filter:brightness(1.07) (mirroring
       .button-gradient:hover) is what actually needs restoring for a gradient
       pill under this hit-target setup. */
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .package-dining-card:hover .package-card-link {
      background-color: var(--bg-h);
      color: var(--fg-h);
      filter: brightness(1.07);
      box-shadow: 0 14px 30px -16px rgba(var(--muted-ink-rgb), 0.4);
      transform: translateY(-1px);
    }
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .package-dining-card:hover .package-card-link::after {
      left: 130%;
    }
  }
  .package-dining-card-figure {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .package-dining-card-figure::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(var(--ink-rgb), 0) 40%,
      rgba(var(--ink-rgb), 0.82)
    );
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .package-dining-card-numeral {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 14px;
    /* Matches .package-dining-card-body's own horizontal padding clamp below,
       so the numeral's left edge lines up with the card copy's left edge. */
    left: clamp(20px, 2vw, 26px);
    z-index: 2;
    font-family: "Fraunces", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(30px, 3.4vw, 44px);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    /* Gold gradient text — same background-clip:text recipe as .on-dark
       .eyebrow, rather than a flat gold. */
    background: var(--gold-gradient);
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    color: transparent;
  }
  .package-dining-card-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex: 1;
    gap: clamp(16px, 2vw, 20px);
    padding: clamp(22px, 2.4vw, 30px) clamp(20px, 2vw, 26px) clamp(24px, 2.6vw, 30px);
  }
  .package-dining-card-body p {
    font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .package-dining-card-tiers {
    /* Pushes the tier list (and the button after it) to the bottom of the
       card once .package-dining-card's height:100% stretches it to match its
       row siblings — sits on top of the gap above, not instead of it. */
    margin-top: auto;
    /* Centered as its own unit (label + names both), rather than following
       the h4/p's left alignment — centering just the names row read as
       misaligned against the label sitting above it. */
    text-align: center;
  }
  .package-dining-card-tiers-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 9.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    /* Gold gradient text, same recipe as .package-dining-card-numeral above —
       a flat --bronze here measured ~3.9:1 contrast against this card's
       --ink-2 background, short of WCAG's 4.5:1 small-text minimum. */
    background: var(--gold-gradient);
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    color: transparent;
  }
  /* Single row, not a stacked list — the three tier names are short enough to
     sit side by side, and a horizontal run reads as "pick one of these three"
     more directly. Divider is a vertical rule *between* names only (never
     framing the whole row), matching this site's usual divided-list
     convention (see .tab-panel li elsewhere). */
  .package-dining-card-tiers ol {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    row-gap: 6px;
  }
  /* One shared rule for all four buttons (Reception/Banquet/High Tea and
     Beverage): full-width, centered, capped. 300px reuses the site's existing
     full-width-CTA convention (.hero-ctas .button etc.) rather than a new
     arbitrary cap. */
  .package-card-link {
    margin-top: 4px;
    align-self: center;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 300px;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  /* The 4px nudge above reads fine after the dining cards' tiers row, but is a
     stray gap after Beverage's own flex gap — zeroed out here instead of
     removing it from the shared rule, so the dining cards keep it. */
  .package-beverage-card-body .package-card-link {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 621px) {
    .package-beverage-card-body .package-card-link {
      align-self: flex-start;
    }
  }
  /* Beverage's own button stays centered (the shared default above) only
     while the dining triptych above it is also single-column — once the
     dining cards move to their 2-up/3-up tier, left-align it instead so it
     matches the card body's own left-aligned h4/p/eyebrow rather than sitting
     centered under left-aligned copy. Moved 700px → 621px with that tier's own
     lower bound, so the two still change over together. */
  /* The "+" joint: makes the additive relationship structural. */
  .package-join {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
    /* Matches .section-head's own margin-bottom (clamp(44px, 6vw, 72px)) so
       the gap above "Then add your bar" equals the gap above "Choose your
       dining experience". */
    margin: clamp(40px, 5vw, 72px) 0 clamp(44px, 6vw, 72px);
  }
  .package-join-line {
    flex: 1;
    height: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      rgba(var(--limestone-rgb), 0.04),
      rgba(var(--gold-rgb), 0.35)
    );
  }
  .package-join-line:last-child {
    background: linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      rgba(var(--gold-rgb), 0.35),
      rgba(var(--limestone-rgb), 0.04)
    );
  }
  .package-join-plus {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 42px;
    height: 42px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--gold-rgb), 0.45);
    color: var(--gold-2);
    font-family: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1;
    flex: none;
  }
  /* Beverage: one wide additive band, deliberately not a fourth card. */
  .package-beverage-card {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    /* Same ground and same resting edge as .package-dining-card
       (2026-09-25) — was linear-gradient(120deg, --ink-3, --ink-2) with a
       gold 0.22 border. The gradient and the gold edge were marking this
       out as a different KIND of card, but the section's numbered steps,
       the "+" joint and the full-width band already say that structurally,
       and three package cards carrying three different surfaces read as
       three unrelated components rather than one family. Distinction now
       comes from shape and position, not from its own palette. */
    background: var(--ink-2);
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--limestone-rgb), 0.12);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    overflow: hidden;
    /* Same [data-reveal] restatement as .package-dining-card above, same
       reason — this card also carries data-reveal. */
    transition:
      opacity 0.9s var(--ease),
      transform 1s var(--ease),
      border-color 0.5s var(--ease);
  }
  @media (hover: none) {
    .package-beverage-card {
      border-color: transparent;
      background:
        linear-gradient(120deg, var(--ink-3), var(--ink-2)) padding-box,
        var(--gold-gradient-soft) border-box;
    }
  }
  /* 0.38, matching .package-dining-card:hover exactly — was 0.5, which
       paired with the stronger resting border this card used to carry. */
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .package-beverage-card:hover {
      border-color: rgba(var(--gold-rgb), 0.38);
    }
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .package-beverage-card:hover .package-card-link {
      background-color: var(--bg-h);
      color: var(--fg-h);
      filter: brightness(1.07);
      box-shadow: 0 14px 30px -16px rgba(var(--muted-ink-rgb), 0.4);
      transform: translateY(-1px);
    }
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .package-beverage-card:hover .package-card-link::after {
      left: 130%;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 621px) {
    .package-beverage-card {
      grid-template-columns: 0.95fr 1.05fr;
      align-items: stretch;
    }
  }
  .package-beverage-card-figure {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* width:100%/max-width:100% caps this back to the grid track's own width
       regardless of what aspect-ratio would otherwise compute from a
       stretched row height — confirmed live at 861px, where the grid
       correctly assigned this column 367px but the rendered element came out
       533px wide (533 = the row's stretched height × 16/11), aspect-ratio
       pulling width back FROM the taller sibling column instead of staying
       capped. min-width:0 kept as the secondary, more usual fix for this
       class of grid-track overflow (see .split > * elsewhere), but it wasn't
       what was actually overflowing here. */
    min-width: 0;
  }
  /* Wide-and-low keeps the band reading as a horizontal add-on strip rather
       than a tall fourth card. */
  @media (min-width: 621px) {
    .package-beverage-card-figure {
      aspect-ratio: 16 / 11;
    }
  }
  .package-beverage-card-body {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: clamp(26px, 3.4vw, 46px) clamp(22px, 3.4vw, 48px);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: clamp(16px, 2vw, 20px);
  }
  .package-beverage-card-eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .package-beverage-card-body p {
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.5vw, 17px);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 44ch;
  }
  /* One source of truth for the thumbnail column: the grid track below and
       the "or" badge's own horizontal centering (further down) both read this,
       so the badge can't drift off the image's centre line if the width is
       ever retuned. Declared on the <li> rather than the card, since the badge
       is the card's sibling, not its child. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-dining-card-item {
      /* The +4px is bought from .package-dining-card-body's right padding
         below, not from the copy: the body is a 1fr track, so widening this
         one narrows that one by exactly the same amount, and trimming the
         padding by 4px hands the copy's usable width straight back. Net effect
         is a slightly larger photo and an identical text column at every
         viewport — the column-gap is deliberately untouched, since that
         separation was tuned on its own merits.

         Written as calc(… + 4px) around the original clamp rather than folded
         into new literals so the borrowed 4px stays visible as a borrowed 4px,
         and reverting is one edit at each end. */
      --package-thumbnail-width: calc(clamp(110px, 34vw, 160px) + 4px);
    }
  }
  /* Image sits flush against the card like .package-beverage-card's own
       figure does — no card-level padding inset around it, corners left
       unrounded on the figure and clipped by the card's own overflow:hidden +
       border-radius. Padding lives on the text column only (below), so the gap
       between image and copy is real column-gap rather than competing with an
       equally-sized outer inset that made it read as barely-there. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-dining-card {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: var(--package-thumbnail-width) 1fr;
      align-items: start;
      column-gap: clamp(18px, 4.5vw, 24px);
    }
  }
  /* No declared ratio here, deliberately: the card's height is the copy
       column's height, full stop, and the image takes whatever that turns out
       to be.

       Any aspect-ratio on this figure acts as a minimum height on the whole
       row, because the figure is a grid item in the same row as the copy — so
       the card could never get shorter than the ratio demanded even when the
       content didn't need the space. That showed as dead space in the copy
       column, and it got worse as the viewport widened: at 620px the track
       hits its 160px cap, and a 2:3 ratio forced a ~240px floor while the
       copy, wrapping to roughly two lines by then, needed about 175px.

       `auto` is stated explicitly rather than just omitted — it has to beat the
       base .package-dining-card-figure rule's own 1/1. It also cancels the
       CLAUDE.md bug #21 mechanism, which needs an aspect-ratio to compute a
       width from; the width caps below are kept anyway as cheap insurance, the
       grid track already being what sets the width. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-dining-card-figure {
      align-self: stretch;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 100%;
      min-width: 0;
    }
  }
  /* The numeral and the figure's bottom scrim both stay at this width. They
       travel together rather than being separable: the numeral is
       gold-gradient text sitting directly on photography, and the dark bottom
       fade is what keeps it legible over a bright plate of food. The shared
       180deg fade works unchanged on a tall, narrow thumbnail.

       Only the numeral's own scale and inset are retuned — the shared rule's
       clamp(30px, 3.4vw, 44px) and clamp(20px, 2vw, 26px) inset were sized
       against a full-width card image and read oversized, pushed too far in,
       on a ~133px thumbnail. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-dining-card-numeral {
      font-size: clamp(24px, 6.5vw, 30px);
      bottom: 10px;
      left: 12px;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-dining-card-body {
      /* Matches .package-beverage-card-body's own min-width:0 — stops the 1fr
         track's automatic `auto` minimum letting this column's least-shrinkable
         content (the button: nowrap label, flex-shrink:0) push the track wider
         than the space actually available. */
      min-width: 0;
      /* Top/right/bottom only — left is 0 since column-gap already separates
         this from the image. The right side is 4px short of the other two:
         that's the 4px handed to --package-thumbnail-width above, given back
         here so the copy's usable width is unchanged by the wider photo. The
         asymmetry is deliberate and invisible — text-to-card-edge is the one
         inset with slack in it, since nothing sits against that edge. */
      padding: clamp(16px, 4vw, 20px) calc(clamp(16px, 4vw, 20px) - 4px)
        clamp(16px, 4vw, 20px) 0;
      /* One flat gap between the body's children (heading, copy, button —
         tiers are hidden at this width). */
      gap: 12px;
    }
  }
  /* A ceiling, not a target: line-clamp only truncates once the text actually
       exceeds the limit, so anything up to five lines renders in full with no
       ellipsis and the descriptions read complete at normal phone widths (the
       current three are ~91-100 characters, landing around four lines at
       ~390px). It only starts biting at the narrow end — around 320px the text
       column is down to ~144px, roughly 22 characters a line, which is where
       the longest description reaches five. So this is a guard against a card
       running away if the copy is ever rewritten longer, not a crop applied to
       today's copy.

       Native -webkit-line-clamp is fine here: CLAUDE.md bug #18 only rules it
       out for *centered* text (where it can silently fail to truncate at all,
       hence the JS-measured approach used for the testimonials quote). This
       copy is left-aligned. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-dining-card-body p {
      display: -webkit-box;
      -webkit-line-clamp: 5;
      -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
      line-clamp: 5;
      overflow: hidden;
    }
  }
  /* Tiers hidden on mobile for now. Kept in the markup rather than removed
       from the HTML so this is one line to reverse and every larger breakpoint
       is untouched — but note display:none drops them from the accessibility
       tree too, so the tier names genuinely aren't available to a screen reader
       at this width, not merely hidden visually. That's fine while it's a
       deliberate content call (the card's own accessible name comes from
       .package-card-hit's aria-label regardless); it would need revisiting if
       the tiers ever became load-bearing information. The body's flex gap
       closes up on its own — a display:none child consumes no gap. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-dining-card-tiers {
      display: none;
    }
  }
  /* Sized down to .button-small's own dimensions (the --button-compact-*
       tokens, reused rather than inventing a new size) so the label fits the
       narrow text column comfortably — the shared full-size .button is what
       CLAUDE.md bug #20 catches here — but stretched edge to edge across that column
       rather than shrink-wrapped, so it lines up with the copy above it. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-card-link {
      height: var(--button-compact-height);
      padding: 0 var(--button-compact-padding-x);
      font-size: var(--button-compact-font-size);
      align-self: stretch;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: none;
      margin-top: 6px;
    }
  }
  /* Beverage never got the thumbnail-left/text-column split above — it
       stays single-column at this width (its own two-column layout only
       starts at 621px+), so the unscoped rule above sees a full-card-width
       parent instead of a narrow text column and stretches edge to edge
       across the whole card, wider than the three dining buttons beside it.
       300px matches the sitewide capped-CTA convention (.package-card-link's
       own base rule, .hero-ctas .button, etc.); centered per request, same
       as the base rule's own default align-self:center — the 621px+ rule
       below switches this to flex-start once the dining triptych above it
       is also left-aligned, but at this single-column mobile width centered
       is what's wanted. Higher specificity than the bare .package-card-link
       above (0,2,0 vs 0,1,0) wins regardless of declaration order. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-beverage-card-body .package-card-link {
      max-width: 300px;
      align-self: center;
    }
  }
  /* The two "or" badges interlock with the images at this width rather than
       dividing whole cards: each straddles the seam between the card above and
       the card below, centred on the thumbnail column so it overlaps the
       bottom of one image and the top of the next.

       Taken out of flow entirely (the <li> is already position:relative
       above), so the list's own grid gap alone sets the distance between cards
       and the badge just sits on top of it — nothing here changes spacing.

       top: half the list's row gap, minus half the badge's 46px height, puts
       the badge's centre exactly on the seam. The gap literal has to match
       .package-dining-card-list's own gap above; the horizontal centring reads
       --package-thumbnail-width (set on the <li>) so it stays locked to the
       real track width.

       z-index 4, not the 2 the other breakpoints use: those sit in the empty
       gap between cards, but this one overlaps the cards themselves, and
       .package-card-hit (the stretched whole-card link) is z-index 3. Neither
       the card nor the <li> opens a stacking context (both position:relative,
       z-index auto), so without this the hit target would take pointer events
       over the badge. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-dining-card-item:nth-child(2) .package-dining-card-or,
    .package-dining-card-item:nth-child(3) .package-dining-card-or {
      position: absolute;
      top: calc(clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 30px) / -2 - 23px);
      left: calc(var(--package-thumbnail-width) / 2);
      transform: translateX(-50%);
      z-index: 4;
    }
  }
  /* Beverage deliberately stays a different shape from the triptych — that
       contrast is existing design intent (a wide band, not a fourth card) and
       it marks the change of domain from choosing a format to choosing a bar.
       The problem was the direction of the contrast, not the contrast itself.

       On desktop Beverage is distinct by being wide and SHORT — a secondary
       strip under the triptych. On mobile it goes single-column, so at 16/10
       the image alone came out ~219px at a ~350px card and the whole band ran
       ~410px: roughly twice a dining card, making the add-on the most dominant
       thing in the section and inverting the hierarchy desktop establishes.
       21/9 brings the image back to ~150px so it reads as a strip again.

       Safe to use a plain aspect-ratio here, unlike the dining figure above:
       this card is single-column at mobile, so the figure sits in its own grid
       row rather than sharing one with the copy. Its width is definite and the
       height derives from it — the normal direction. CLAUDE.md bug #21 needs
       the opposite and can't arise. Note the banner still scales with width,
       so it is tallest at the top of this range (~249px at 620px, vs ~362px
       before); worth a max-height if that end ever looks heavy, left off for
       now rather than adding a second lever to reason about. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-beverage-card-figure {
      aspect-ratio: 21 / 9;
    }
  }
  /* "Your bar, your way" hidden at this width. It's the only eyebrow on a
       card in this section — the dining cards don't carry one — so on mobile,
       where the band is trimmed back to a strip anyway, it reads as an extra
       line of chrome above a heading that already says Beverage.

       Specificity checked rather than assumed (CLAUDE.md bug #14): the element
       carries both .eyebrow and .package-beverage-card-eyebrow, and the only
       `display` in play is .eyebrow's own `flex` at the same 0,1,0 weight, so
       this wins on source order — .eyebrow is declared near the top of this
       file, this block near the end. `.on-dark .eyebrow` is higher (0,2,0) but
       only sets the gold gradient, no display, so it doesn't interfere. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .package-beverage-card-eyebrow {
      display: none;
    }
  }
  /* The compact/pointer instances are plain text, not a boxed callout — the
     status color/dot on .availability-picked-status below already carries the same information
     a card's border-left accent used to (the showcase's own card is restored
     separately via .availability-intro .availability-picked above).
     Show/hide is a hard display:none/flex cut, not animated — an entrance/exit
     fade was attempted and pulled back out after repeated attempts at getting
     it smooth on the showcase's #availSelection instance didn't pan out (it
     also carries data-reveal for its own one-time scroll-in fade, which kept
     conflicting with any opacity-based show/hide state). The transition below
     is only for .swapping (switching between two already-visible picks), which
     never touches display and was never part of the problem. */
  .availability-picked {
    margin-top: 20px;
    display: none;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 16px;
    transition: opacity 0.2s var(--ease);
  }
  .availability-picked.show {
    display: flex;
  }
  .availability-picked.swapping {
    opacity: 0.35;
  }
  .availability-picked-info {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 5px;
  }
  .availability-picked-label {
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bronze);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .availability-picked-date {
    font-family: "Fraunces", serif;
    font-size: 19px;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  :global(.availability.compact) .availability-picked {
    margin-top: 20px;
    gap: 4px;
  }
  :global(.availability.compact) .availability-picked-date {
    font-size: 16px;
  }
  /* ---------- Availability calendar: premium "Venue Diary" treatment ----------
     #availability on weddings.html. Reuses the shared .availability widget in main.js
     completely unchanged (same class hooks it always looked for: .availability-month,
     .availability-prev/.availability-next, .availability-grid, .availability-picked) — this only restyles the
     wrapper via a .diary modifier and adds the always-visible intro column (a plain
     hint before a date is picked, the real .availability-picked panel once one is). */
  .availability-intro {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 22px;
  }
  /* Unlike the compact/pointer instances (plain text, no card — see
     .availability.compact .availability-picked above), the showcase's "Your selection" panel
     keeps the boxed-card treatment: background, border, status-colored left
     accent, shadow. Only shown once a date's picked (.show — see main.js),
     same as every other .availability-picked instance — no longer forced visible via
     .always with a placeholder. */
  .availability-intro .availability-picked {
    /* transition intentionally not re-declared here — the base .availability-picked
       rule already lists border-left-color alongside opacity/transform, and two
       same-property transition declarations at different specificities have the
       more specific one win wholesale, not per-property (bug #2 in CLAUDE.md),
       which would silently drop the transform entrance animation on this card. */
    margin-top: 0;
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    background: #fff;
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--muted-ink-rgb), 0.08);
    border-left: 3px solid rgba(var(--muted-ink-rgb), 0.18);
    box-shadow: 0 10px 24px -18px rgba(var(--muted-ink-rgb), 0.22);
  }
  .availability-intro .availability-picked.status-open {
    border-left-color: var(--avail-green-fg);
  }
  .availability-intro .availability-picked.status-interest {
    border-left-color: var(--avail-amber-fg);
  }
  .availability-select-note {
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted-ink);
    font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(var(--muted-ink-rgb), 0.1);
    padding-top: 16px;
  }
  .availability.diary {
    /* 480px + 20px, matching .availability's own padding increase above (max raised
       10px per side) — keeps the same inner content width the day-grid had
       before, rather than the extra padding eating into it. */
    max-width: 500px;
    border-radius: calc(var(--radius-card) * 2);
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px -30px rgba(var(--muted-ink-rgb), 0.32);
  }
  /* Reverts to the original 480px at true mobile — the padding increase
       above is already excluded there (unchanged clamp min), so there's no
       extra padding to account for at this width either. */
  @media (max-width: 620.98px) {
    .availability.diary {
      max-width: 480px;
    }
  }
  .availability.diary .availability-foot {
    margin-top: 24px;
    padding-top: 22px;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(var(--muted-ink-rgb), 0.1);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .availability-foot-note {
    display: none;
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted-ink);
    font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif;
  }
  /* Shown once a date's picked — mirrors .availability-select-note's copy, which used to
     be the only place this reassurance text lived (inside the section's own
     .availability-intro callout). :has() keys off the same status class the dot above
     uses, so no extra JS is needed just to toggle this line. */
  .availability-foot:has(.availability-foot-label.status-open) + .availability-foot-note,
  .availability-foot:has(.availability-foot-label.status-interest)
    + .availability-foot-note {
    display: block;
  }
  @media (min-width: 981px) {
    :global(#availability) .availability-foot-note {
      display: none;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .availability-foot {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      text-align: center;
      gap: 20px;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .availability-foot-note {
      margin-top: 20px;
    }
  }
  /* ---------- Quick-pick calendar modal (weddings.html only) ----------
     Reuses .availability.diary + .availability-foot unchanged inside a fixed overlay — same
     show/hide technique as .lightbox below (opacity+visibility with a matched
     transition-delay, not display:none, so it can actually fade — see main.js
     for why display-based show/hide caused so much trouble on .availability-picked). */
  .availability-modal {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 970;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 24px;
    overflow-y: auto;
    background: rgba(10, 9, 6, 0.7);
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition:
      opacity 0.35s var(--ease),
      visibility 0s linear 0.35s;
  }
  .availability-modal.show {
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    transition:
      opacity 0.35s var(--ease),
      visibility 0s linear 0s;
  }
  .availability-modal-backdrop {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }
  .availability-modal-panel {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 480px;
    margin: auto;
    padding-top: 48px;
  }
  .availability-modal-close {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: transparent;
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--limestone-rgb), 0.4);
    color: var(--limestone);
    cursor: pointer;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    transition:
      background 0.3s var(--ease),
      color 0.3s var(--ease),
      border-color 0.3s var(--ease);
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .availability-modal-close:hover {
      background: var(--gold);
      color: var(--ink);
      border-color: var(--gold);
    }
  }
  @media (hover: none) {
    .availability-modal-close:active {
      background: var(--gold);
      color: var(--ink);
      border-color: var(--gold);
    }
  }
  .availability-modal-panel .availability.diary {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .availability-modal-done {
    flex: none;
  }
  .availability-modal-done:disabled {
    opacity: 0.3;
    cursor: auto;
    /* .button's hover (translateY + box-shadow) and ::after sheen sweep, plus its
       :active scale-down, aren't guarded by :disabled anywhere in the shared
       .button rules (styles.css) — browsers don't reliably suppress :hover/:active
